WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.789
The most famous news story of all time unfolded
behind me, the headquarters of the Washington

00:00:10.789 --> 00:00:11.789
Post.

00:00:11.789 --> 00:00:16.582
The story was the Watergate scandal, and the
Post reporting on it helped bring down a president.

00:00:16.582 --> 00:00:23.400
The story began when five men were caught
burglarizing the Democratic National headquarters

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.890
in the Watergate complex.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:29.830
As it happened, they had links to President
Nixon's reelection campaign.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:34.980
Their capture sparked a flurry of news coverage,
but then it quickly died down.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.769
Five months later, Richard Nixon was elected
to the presidency by a landslide, and the

00:00:39.769 --> 00:00:44.880
break in at the Watergate complex seemed almost
like a footnote to the campaign.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.080
And it might have remained there, except for
two young Washington Post reporters, Bob Woodward

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.940
and Carl Bernstein.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:57.019
They pursued lead after lead, revealing in
one instance that Attorney General John Mitchell

00:00:57.019 --> 00:01:01.390
had a secret fund that was used to spy on
democratic officials.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.671
The Nixon administration charged that their
reporting was biased and baseless, and they

00:01:06.671 --> 00:01:11.850
pressured the Post publisher, Katharine Graham,
and the Post editor, Ben Bradlee, to call

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.109
off the two young reporters.

00:01:14.109 --> 00:01:17.720
Graham and Bradley refused, but they had their
doubts.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.960
If what Woodward and Bernstein was reporting
was so accurate and so important, why were

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.850
other news organizations not following up
on the story?

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:32.109
Eventually the Post reporting convinced Congress
that there was something to this story and

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:37.450
Congress began its investigation that in the
end led to the resignation of President Richard

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.880
Nixon.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.200
The Post Watergate coverage illustrates the
press at its best, pursuing its watchdog role

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.319
under the First Amendment, carrying out its
duty to keep an eye on those in power, and

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:53.789
to reporting when they engage in wrongdoing.

